# SEC-004 Trivy container-scan allow-list.
#
# .trivyignore is Trivy's native expiring-ignore mechanism (default ignorefile
# path, picked up automatically by `trivy image` with no extra flag). An entry
# with `exp:YYYY-MM-DD` stops applying once that date passes — the finding it
# covers starts failing the "Trivy scan (fail on fixable CRITICAL/HIGH)" step
# again. This mirrors scripts/ci/cve-allowlist.json's expiring-allow-list
# convention (SEC-004): tracked debt, not a blanket approval.
#
# Generated 2026-09-09 by building the 3 matrix'd images
# (gateway-service/finance-service/hrms-service) from this exact Dockerfile
# and running the exact CI scan (`trivy image --severity CRITICAL,HIGH
# --ignore-unfixed`) against each. Two real, quick fixes were made first
# (see Dockerfile comments) and removed the vast majority of what Trivy found:
#   - Debian OS-layer CRITICAL/HIGH CVEs (libgnutls30, libcap2) had
#     already-published point-release fixes; added `apt-get upgrade -y`.
#     Result: 6 -> 0 OS CVEs per image.
#   - The "prune" stage was not actually producing a production-only
#     node_modules (pnpm quirk: `pnpm install --prod` on top of an
#     already-fully-installed node_modules does not retroactively strip
#     devDependencies) — every runtime image shipped every service's
#     dev/test/build tooling. Rebuilt the stage to install fresh. Result:
#     stryker/vitest/eslint/turbo/prettier and ~40 transitive dev-tooling CVEs
#     gone per image.
#   - The base node image's bundled npm CLI (never invoked at runtime) carried
#     its own bundled tar/pacote/sigstore/ip-address/minimatch/cross-spawn.
#     Removed it. Result: that whole category gone per image.
#
# What's left below (identical across all 3 images) are real, fixable
# CRITICAL/HIGH CVEs in packages that ARE genuine production dependencies
# (fastify, find-my-way, next, drizzle-orm) or their own transitive deps
# (brace-expansion, browserslist, fast-uri, js-yaml). Every one of these
# packages already has a live, reasoned, expiring entry in
# scripts/ci/cve-allowlist.json (the pnpm-audit-based SEC-004 gate) requiring
# the same fleet-wide major/minor version bump, tracked under follow-up gap
# SEC-018. This file reuses those exact expiry dates so the two gates
# (pnpm-audit CVE gate and this Trivy scan) stay honest and in sync rather
# than drifting.
#
# NOTE: cve-allowlist.json's reason text for brace-expansion/browserslist/
# fast-uri/js-yaml previously said "not shipped in any runtime image" (dev-
# tooling only). This Trivy scan disproves that for these 4 — they ARE
# present in the shipped runtime image as transitive production dependencies.
# Corrected in cve-allowlist.json's reason text in this same fixup; the
# remediation path (SEC-018, same expiry) is unchanged.

# fastify 4.29.1 -> needs >=5.7.2 (SEC-018, tied to REL-016)
